# The full container run at c8104865: 39 gates, 31 594 tests, nothing failed (2026-10-04)

`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --full` was run end to end against the committed tree at
`c8104865` and passed: 39 gates, 386 test-result blocks, 31 594 tests passed, 485 ignored,
0 failed, exit 0. This is the successor to
`docs/verification/verify-in-docker-full-a04e91a7-2026-10-04.log`; the counts moved because
three gates and one test were added between the two commits. It is the record behind the
`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` row of `scripts/ci/docker-labs.tsv`.

The full 3.0 MB / 36 202-line transcript is not committed. Every line quoted below is copied
out of it, and section 6 gives the commands to reproduce it and to re-measure the counts.

== 0. Why the run sits in its own clone

`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` bind-mounts the repository root it was invoked from and
fingerprints the working tree once before the gates and once after; on any movement it prints
`UNATTRIBUTABLE: the source tree changed while the gates ran.` and exits 1. So the run went to
a self-contained clone rather than the live checkout:

    $ git clone <this repository> /var/tmp/labcov-full-c8104865
    $ cd /var/tmp/labcov-full-c8104865 && git checkout --detach c8104865
    $ git status --porcelain | wc -l
    0
    $ ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --full > /var/tmp/labcov-full-c8104865.log 2>&1
    $ echo "exit=$?"
    exit=0

`git worktree` is not an option: the script's first action is a preflight in which the
container reads the mounted `/src` with git, and a worktree's `.git` is a text pointer to a
file inside the parent repository that does not exist inside the container. A clone carries its
own object store, so the preflight passes. The empty `git status` before the run is what makes
the checksum below a claim about `c8104865` and not about a working state.

== 1. The verdict, as printed

    == building chaos-verify:local from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
    == preparing cargo cache volumes
    == source tree: /var/tmp/labcov-full-c8104865 at c8104865: 4027 files, checksum 528558864

    == preflight: container can read the repository
    preflight: OK

    == toolchain matches the pin
    rustc 1.94.0 (4a4ef493e 2026-03-02)
    cargo 1.94.0 (85eff7c80 2026-01-15)

    ...

    GUI protocol types are up to date

    all gates passed in chaos-verify:local

The transcript never prints `UNATTRIBUTABLE`. The script recomputes the same checksum after the
last gate and prints that sentence only on a mismatch, so its absence is the statement that all
39 gates, and the 31 594 tests inside them, belong to the tree whose checksum is 528558864 and
that no file moved while they ran.

The transcript was created at 18:18:16 and last written at 18:43:12, so 1 496 seconds
(24 minutes 56 seconds). That is not a cold-cache figure: the cargo registry, the cargo git
checkouts and `target/` live in three named volumes that earlier runs had already filled, and
`chaos-verify:local` already existed. On a clean machine the same command takes considerably
longer and this figure should not be read as a baseline.

== 2. What ran, and how the gate count is derived

`--full` does not print a gate count, so the number is derived rather than quoted. The script
holds 37 gates as literal entries in its `gates=()` array and appends two more only in full
mode, after `cargo test` has already built what they need:

    $ awk 'NR>230 && /^\)/{exit} NR>230 && /^  "/{n++} END{print n+0}' scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
    37
    $ grep -n 'gates+=(' scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
    317:  gates+=("cargo test: ${bootstrap}; cargo test --workspace --locked --no-fail-fast")
    323:  gates+=("GUI protocol types: ${bootstrap}; bash scripts/ci/check-gui-protocol.sh")

Those two are inside `if [ "${MODE}" = "full" ]`, so a `--full` run executes 39 gates. The
transcript carries 46 lines starting with `== `, which accounts for exactly that: 39 gates,
4 preamble lines (`building`, `preparing cargo cache volumes`, `source tree:`, `preflight:`)
and 3 sub-headings that two gates print for themselves (`version lockstep` prints two,
`installer guards` prints one). Diffing the 46 headings against the 39 labels leaves those
nine and nothing else, which is the check that no gate silently ran twice or not at all.

Three gates print a count worth recording, because they read the whole tree:

    ignored-test baseline: 429 total, 0 approved bare, 0 new
    ignored-tests: baseline matches; 429 ignored attributes total
    baseline holds: 97 crates, 0 fewer production sites than recorded
    uncompiled set holds: 0 files, exactly as recorded
    metric labels hold: 83 registrations, 155 label-value call sites, 0 recorded
    timeout children hold: 12 timeout-abandoned output/status site(s), 12 kill their child
      on drop, 0 are std commands, 0 recorded
    tree-ownership: OK (4693 path(s) walked under src, all owned by 1003, 1 path(s) skipped
      as pruned or mounted)
    check-lab-coverage: OK (6 lab(s): 3 run by CI, 3 with a current ledger row)

== 3. The three cargo legs, and the GUI check

Those four legs are the expensive ones and the ones no host-side command reaches:

    == cargo check    Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 2m 26s
    == cargo clippy   Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 3m 00s
    == cargo test     386 `test result:` blocks, counted in section 4
    == GUI protocol types
    GUI protocol types are up to date

`cargo clippy` runs with `-D warnings`, and the region between its heading and the next carries
zero lines starting with `warning`:

    $ awk '/^== cargo clippy/,/^== cargo test/' /var/tmp/labcov-full-c8104865.log | grep -c '^warning'
    0

That matters for how section 4 should be read: the test counts below come from a tree that also
compiles clean under the lint set, so a passing test is not standing in for a build problem
somewhere else.

== 4. The size of the test leg

    $ grep -c "^test result: ok" /var/tmp/labcov-full-c8104865.log
    386
    $ grep -oE "^test result: ok\. [0-9]+ passed; [0-9]+ failed; [0-9]+ ignored" \
          /var/tmp/labcov-full-c8104865.log | awk -F'[ ;]+' \
          '{p+=$4; f+=$6; i+=$8} END {print "passed:", p, " failed:", f, " ignored:", i}'
    passed: 31594  failed: 0  ignored: 485
    $ grep -c "^test result: FAILED" /var/tmp/labcov-full-c8104865.log
    0

The leg is invoked with `--no-fail-fast`, so "0 failed" is a conclusion drawn after all 386
blocks ran rather than a sweep that stopped at the first red test.

The 485 ignored break down as 436 unit and integration tests plus 49 doctests, which cargo
reports in the same line shape:

    $ grep "\.\.\. ignored" /var/tmp/labcov-full-c8104865.log | grep -v "\.rs - " | wc -l
    436
    $ grep -E "^test [^ ]+\.rs - .* \.\.\. ignored" /var/tmp/labcov-full-c8104865.log | wc -l
    49

That is a different population from the `ignored-test baseline` gate's "429 total", which counts
`#[ignore]` attributes in the tracked sources and so sees neither the doctests nor an attribute
the scan cannot attach to a test function. The two numbers answer different questions.

== 5. Five fixtures skipped inside the container, and what each one still leaves covered

The run is green, but four of its own fixture suites declined to check five cases, and all five
are worth stating rather than leaving inside an `OK (skipped=N)`:

    Ran 9 tests in 0.358s
    OK (skipped=1)                                    workflow yaml
        skipped 'PyYAML is not installed on this host'
    Ran 28 tests in 0.812s
    OK (skipped=1)                                    tree ownership
        skipped 'root reads a 000 directory, so the branch cannot be reached here'
    Ran 33 tests in 16.970s
    OK (skipped=1)                                    metric labels
        skipped 'the prometheus source is not vendored on this host'
    Ran 38 tests in 28.634s
    OK (skipped=2)                                    timeout children
        skipped 'the pinned tokio source is not vendored on this machine'   (x2)

None of the five is a hole this commit could close by editing a test. Two depend on a vendored
crate source the image does not carry (`prometheus` for the metric-labels premise, `tokio` for
the two timeout-children premises); one depends on PyYAML, which the image deliberately omits so
that `check-workflow-yaml.py`'s own line-oriented reader stays the thing under test; one depends
on not running as root, which the container does. All five run where their precondition holds,
which is why the premises they check are still checked somewhere. The root-dependent one is
stated inside the fixture itself rather than assumed: the container user is root, so a `chmod
000` directory is readable and the branch that reports "cannot read the tree" cannot be reached
at all, in this container or in CI.

== 6. Reproducing it, and what this record is not

    $ git clone <this repository> /var/tmp/labcov-full-c8104865
    $ cd /var/tmp/labcov-full-c8104865 && git checkout --detach c8104865
    $ ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --full

What the run does not cover:

- This is `c8104865`, two commits before the installer stall floor. That change and its
  fixtures are recorded in `docs/verification/installer-download-stall-2026-10-04.log`, which
  also records the container leg that does cover them (`--only "installer guards"`).
- Not a cold cache, and not a baseline. See section 1 about the named volumes.
- Linux only. The entry point is a Debian bookworm-slim container; the macOS and Windows
  conclusions come from their own runners, recorded in
  `docs/verification/platform-ci-2026-10-02.log` and
  `docs/verification/windows-test-failures-2026-10-04.log`.
- No browser, and no network egress from the gates themselves. Nothing in the 39 gates drives a
  UI, and the image has no node_modules and no browser binary, so "all gates passed" and "the
  TUI and the desktop flows behave" remain two separate conclusions.
